From 04aebf90c8986350e2ebedcfa47d84fe1522b21b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Isaac Duarte <31712685+Isaac-Duarte@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 15:51:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 32/32] phy: apple: atc-t6050: keep the PHY's domains through
 suspend-to-idle

On the J714s both Type-C ports are dead after a suspend-to-idle wake. A
dock plugged in after it fails on either port before the PHY firmware is
reached:

  apple-t6050-dp-phy 2ab000000.phy: start of mode 1 failed (-110)
  apple-t6050-dp-phy 383000000.phy: CIO mode failed (-110), reverse orientation, 1 ms

and the port controllers then give up ("failed to place Type-C mux in
safe mode: -5"). The same dock comes up in CIO mode on both ports after a
fresh boot. It happened in three boots: in one (08:25) the ports had not
been used before the sleep and both failed after it; in another (09:02)
both had run in CIO mode before it, and the right port, the only one
tried, failed after it; the third (12:03) is the traced run below.

The display crossbar holds each port's usb-aon and common domains from
its probe on, so those never switch off at runtime. genpd's noirq
suspend gates a domain once every member device has suspended, runtime
holds or not, so suspend-to-idle switches them off for the first time
since boot. No firmware runs at an s2idle wake to set them up again, and
the PHY has no s2idle callbacks: its cold start then times out in the
first power-up step (1 ms, one 1000 us poll).

Traced with the PHY module from before this change (pmgr-pwrstate and
genpd dynamic debug): at the noirq suspend genpd gates atc2_usb, then
atc2_common and atc2_usb_aon, then the same three for ATC1, and powers
all six on again at the resume; each domain's genpd usage goes from 0 to
1. The usb domain was off when genpd took it over (about 5 ms idle at
boot, usage 0); the controller powered it up after boot. So, per
pmgr-pwrstate's rule (GENPD_FLAG_ACTIVE_WAKEUP only for a domain on at
probe), it lacks that flag, and the dwc3 glue in it is counted as
suspended although its wakeup is enabled. The domain goes off when its
last device, a DART, has suspended. Its parents, common and usb-aon,
go off in the same cascade, as this, their last subdomain still on,
goes off: their own devices (the crossbar's and the PHY's) had already
been counted suspended. On a T8142 J813, whose ATC domains were on at
boot, dwc3's wakeup keeps them all on through s2idle.

Hold the PHY's domain devices out of the system transition when the
sleep is suspend-to-idle, from the PM notifier's PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE to
PM_POST_SUSPEND, so that they cover the prepare and complete phases as
well. genpd then never counts a domain's devices all suspended, and each
domain stays as runtime PM had it: an idle port's wrapper off, its usb
domain as the USB controller holds it, the crossbar's on, a port in use
as it was. thunderbolt/apple uses the same syscore mark for its
router's domains, for a cable's lifetime.

The hold is in the PHY rather than the crossbar or the device tree: the
PHY is the device whose cold start needs the domains not gated since
boot, and it already has the J714s sleep notifier. Which of the three
gated domains (usb, common, usb-aon) holds what the PHY needs is not
known, and the hold covers all three. Holding usb is a behaviour change
of its own: before, atc*_usb, the dwc3 and its DARTs went off in s2idle;
now they stay on, as dwc3-apple's s2idle path, which keeps the host in
place, expects. The wrapper and the tunnel clock gates are held too and
stay as runtime PM has them, off on an idle port. apple,sleep-retained
would apply to deep sleep as well, where the PHY's own sleep handling
decides what the always-on domain keeps. Giving the usb domain
GENPD_FLAG_ACTIVE_WAKEUP, as the J813's domains have, would keep the
chain on only while the dwc3 glue's wakeup stays enabled, which user
space can turn off; it would need a new pmgr-pwrstate policy, since the
flag follows the domain's state at probe on every SoC, and it would
change deep sleep for those domains too. device_set_awake_path() on the
PHY's domain devices would keep only domains with that flag, which usb
lacks. The hold states the PHY's own requirement, whatever the wakeup
policy, as the SEP (sep_pm_keep_domains()), the ISP's resident firmware
and the USB4 router (thunderbolt/apple) hold theirs with the same
syscore mark.

Suspend-to-idle on the J714s needs "PCI: apple: qualify the J714s for
suspend-to-idle over the adopted N1 link"; before it every attempt was
refused, so this path had not run there. As with the PHY's deep-sleep
notifier, this is in force on the J714s only, where the notifier is
registered. A suspend-to-idle reached by writing "freeze" while "mem"
means deep is not covered: the notifier goes by the default sleep, as
dwc3-apple's does.

Signed-off-by: Isaac Duarte <31712685+Isaac-Duarte@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/phy/apple/atc-t6050-combo.h | 47 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 drivers/phy/apple/atc-t6050-dp.c    |  1 +
 2 files changed, 41 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/phy/apple/atc-t6050-combo.h b/drivers/phy/apple/atc-t6050-combo.h
index 2e5b8317f1ed..4ff849abb268 100644
--- a/drivers/phy/apple/atc-t6050-combo.h
+++ b/drivers/phy/apple/atc-t6050-combo.h
@@ -118,8 +118,9 @@ static void combo_pipe_fault(struct t6050_dp *dp, const char *what, int ret)
 /*
  * What the combo PHY gained for a deep sleep outside its sleep callbacks (a wrapper domain
  * still held at a stop is released, a safe state with a domain still held is stopped again,
- * the always-on domain's hold over the sleep) is in force on the board it has run on, the
- * J714s. On another T6050 board the stop, the safe state and the probe are as before.
+ * the always-on domain's hold over the sleep) and its domains' hold over suspend-to-idle
+ * are in force on the board they have run on, the J714s. On another T6050 board the stop,
+ * the safe state and the probe are as before.
  */
 static bool combo_sleep_board(void)
 {
@@ -368,9 +369,34 @@ static void combo_resume(struct t6050_dp *dp)
 }
 
 /*
- * A port in use keeps its always-on domain through a deep sleep: held out of the system
- * transition from before its prepare to after its complete, so the power domain is not
- * counted as suspended, and asked of the sleep driver, which lowers the word otherwise.
+ * Suspend-to-idle runs no firmware at the wake, and the PHY has no s2idle callbacks: its
+ * domains have to come out of it as they went in. The display crossbar holds the always-on
+ * and common domains from its probe on, so they never switch off at runtime, but genpd's
+ * noirq suspend gates a domain once every member device has suspended, runtime holds or
+ * not. On the J714s it gates usb (off at probe, so without GENPD_FLAG_ACTIVE_WAKEUP, and
+ * the wakeup of the dwc3 in it does not count), then common and always-on, its parents.
+ * Which of the three held the state the PHY needs is not known; after it, the PHY's first
+ * power-up handshake times out (-ETIMEDOUT) on both ports until a reboot. Hold every domain
+ * of the PHY out of the transition, from before its prepare to after its complete, so
+ * genpd never counts them all suspended: each stays in its runtime state. The usb domain,
+ * and with it the dwc3 and its DARTs, then stays on through s2idle, as dwc3-apple's
+ * s2idle path, which keeps the host in place, expects.
+ */
+static void combo_s2idle_hold(struct t6050_dp *dp, bool hold)
+{
+	for (unsigned int i = 0; i < dp->ndomains; i++)
+		dev_pm_syscore_device(dp->domain[i].dev, hold);
+	for (unsigned int i = 0; dp->dp_gates && i < ATC_DP_TUNNELS; i++)
+		dev_pm_syscore_device(dp->dp_gate[i].dev, hold);
+	dp->s2idle_held = hold;
+	dev_dbg(dp->dev, "domains %s suspend-to-idle\n", hold ? "held out of" : "released after");
+}
+
+/*
+ * Suspend-to-idle holds every domain of the PHY, see combo_s2idle_hold(). In a deep sleep a
+ * port in use keeps its always-on domain: held out of the system transition from before its
+ * prepare to after its complete, so the power domain is not counted as suspended, and asked
+ * of the sleep driver, which lowers the word otherwise.
  */
 static int combo_pm_notify(struct notifier_block *nb, unsigned long action, void *unused)
 {
@@ -379,7 +405,12 @@ static int combo_pm_notify(struct notifier_block *nb, unsigned long action, void
 	guard(mutex)(&dp->lock);
 	switch (action) {
 	case PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE:
-		if (pm_suspend_default_s2idle() || dp->faulted || dp->aon_syscore ||
+		if (pm_suspend_default_s2idle()) {
+			if (!dp->s2idle_held)
+				combo_s2idle_hold(dp, true);
+			break;
+		}
+		if (dp->faulted || dp->aon_syscore ||
 		    dp->combo_mode == COMBO_SAFE || !dp->domain[0].active)
 			break;
 		if (!apple_sleep_usb_aon_keep(dp->port, true))
@@ -388,6 +419,8 @@ static int combo_pm_notify(struct notifier_block *nb, unsigned long action, void
 		dp->aon_syscore = true;
 		break;
 	case PM_POST_SUSPEND:
+		if (dp->s2idle_held)
+			combo_s2idle_hold(dp, false);
 		apple_sleep_usb_aon_keep(dp->port, false);
 		if (dp->aon_syscore) {
 			dev_pm_syscore_device(dp->domain[0].dev, false);
@@ -667,7 +700,7 @@ static int combo_setup(struct platform_device *pdev, struct t6050_dp *dp)
 			return PTR_ERR(dp->combo_phy[i]);
 		phy_set_drvdata(dp->combo_phy[i], dp);
 	}
-	/* A deep sleep's hold of the always-on domain: see combo_sleep_board(). */
+	/* The domains' hold over a system sleep, deep or s2idle: see combo_sleep_board(). */
 	if (!combo_sleep_board())
 		return 0;
 	dp->pm_nb.notifier_call = combo_pm_notify;
diff --git a/drivers/phy/apple/atc-t6050-dp.c b/drivers/phy/apple/atc-t6050-dp.c
index 55c9e3cff2b2..75fdf78c490e 100644
--- a/drivers/phy/apple/atc-t6050-dp.c
+++ b/drivers/phy/apple/atc-t6050-dp.c
@@ -136,6 +136,7 @@ struct t6050_dp {
 	bool sleep_stopped;		/* stopped for a sleep that has not been accounted for yet */
 	bool aon_syscore;		/* the always-on domain is held out of this system transition */
 	bool aon_kept;			/* the stop left the always-on domain's reference in place */
+	bool s2idle_held;		/* every domain is held out of this suspend-to-idle */
 };
 
 static int atc_domain_set(struct atc_domain *pd, bool active)
-- 
2.56.0

